WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.535
-Our next guest is
a four-time Olympian,

00:00:02.535 --> 00:00:04.937
Olympic Gold medalist,
World Cup champion,

00:00:04.937 --> 00:00:07.640
and the most decorated
female skier of all time.

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:11.177
Her memoir, "Rise: My Story,"
is on sale now.

00:00:11.177 --> 00:00:13.379
Please welcome back to the show
our friend, Lindsey Vonn.

00:00:13.379 --> 00:00:15.181
How are you, Lindsey?

00:00:15.181 --> 00:00:17.182
-Hey, Seth, I'm good.
How are you?

00:00:17.182 --> 00:00:18.384
-I'm wonderful.

00:00:18.384 --> 00:00:20.486
I'm very sorry we can't
be doing this in person.

00:00:20.486 --> 00:00:22.555
I do hope next time
that is the case.

00:00:22.555 --> 00:00:24.490
Congratulations on the book.

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:25.724
I want to ask you about that,

00:00:25.724 --> 00:00:27.093
but I feel like
there's something

00:00:27.093 --> 00:00:29.262
even more important
to congratulate you on.

00:00:29.262 --> 00:00:32.065
You went on vacation,
a ski vacation,

00:00:32.065 --> 00:00:34.033
which I think people would
assume you do all the time,

00:00:34.033 --> 00:00:37.370
but you wrote that it was
the first ski vacation

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:39.806
since you were 9 years old.

00:00:39.806 --> 00:00:41.574
-Yeah, I'm pretty pumped
about it.

00:00:41.574 --> 00:00:43.076
It was better than I expected.

00:00:43.076 --> 00:00:44.410
You know, when I was 9,

00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:47.614
I didn't do après-ski,
so I felt like I --

00:00:47.614 --> 00:00:49.982
I crushed the après-ski
this time around,

00:00:49.982 --> 00:00:53.352
and it was a much more
entertaining experience

00:00:53.352 --> 00:00:56.289
than what I remember
when I was 9.

00:00:56.289 --> 00:00:57.757
-Now, a couple of questions.

00:00:57.757 --> 00:00:59.259
You were skiing
with some friends.

00:00:59.259 --> 00:01:01.060
Do you have to, like,
let up a little bit

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:02.395
when you're Lindsey Vonn out

00:01:02.395 --> 00:01:05.197
with some civilians
on their skis?

00:01:05.197 --> 00:01:07.800
-I usually let them have, like,
a little bit of a head start,

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:10.303
and then, when they're about
three-quarters of the way down,

00:01:10.303 --> 00:01:12.505
then I'll try to catch up,
and usually,

00:01:12.505 --> 00:01:15.508
I'll still end up waiting
for them at the lift. [ Laughs ]

00:01:15.508 --> 00:01:17.277
-Now, how many people
on the slopes --

00:01:17.277 --> 00:01:20.880
'Cause I imagine if you see
Lindsey Vonn on the ski slope,

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:22.748
you probably think
you're hallucinating.

00:01:22.748 --> 00:01:24.417
Do people clock you

00:01:24.417 --> 00:01:27.687
when they're next to you
on the ski slopes?

00:01:27.687 --> 00:01:31.257
-I've definitely gotten
some interesting comments.

00:01:31.257 --> 00:01:33.659
I think most of the time,
you know,

00:01:33.659 --> 00:01:35.261
they have their heads turned,
and they're like,

00:01:35.261 --> 00:01:36.495
"What?
What's going on here?"

00:01:36.495 --> 00:01:39.933
And I try to ski a bit incognito
so no one --

00:01:39.933 --> 00:01:42.868
I try not to, you know, draw
too much attention to myself.

00:01:42.868 --> 00:01:46.473
But I definitely catch
the attention of the ski patrol,

00:01:46.473 --> 00:01:48.040
and generally
they're chasing after me,

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:49.642
trying to pull my pass.
[ Laughs ]

00:01:49.642 --> 00:01:50.743
-[ Laughs ]

00:01:50.743 --> 00:01:52.911
I want to also ask
about the fact

00:01:52.911 --> 00:01:55.948
that the Olympics are coming up,
and I'm wondering,

00:01:55.948 --> 00:01:58.217
when it's right
around the corner like this,

00:01:58.217 --> 00:02:00.352
do you feel relief
to not be competing,

00:02:00.352 --> 00:02:04.357
or do you feel nostalgia
for all the years that you did?

00:02:04.357 --> 00:02:06.191
-I definitely have
a bit of FOMO.

00:02:06.191 --> 00:02:07.560
I wish that I was racing,

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:10.162
but at the same time,
I think, right now, with COVID,

00:02:10.162 --> 00:02:15.468
everything is so different and
there's so many new challenges

00:02:15.468 --> 00:02:18.037
that I definitely
am not envious of that.

00:02:18.037 --> 00:02:20.072
But I do miss the competition.

00:02:20.072 --> 00:02:23.009
And, you know, the Olympics
are always so special.

00:02:23.009 --> 00:02:24.710
You know, the opening ceremonies

00:02:24.710 --> 00:02:27.413
and -- and just
the whole experience in general.

00:02:27.413 --> 00:02:29.182
I'm definitely
going to be jealous,

00:02:29.182 --> 00:02:31.050
but I'm excited
to cheer on my teammates,

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:34.954
my former teammates, and
all of the Team USA athletes.

00:02:34.954 --> 00:02:36.922
It's going to be fun to watch.

00:02:36.922 --> 00:02:39.292
-Now, I can't quite remember
where skiing falls

00:02:39.292 --> 00:02:40.926
in the winter Olympics,
but did you --

00:02:40.926 --> 00:02:42.161
after you competed,

00:02:42.161 --> 00:02:43.930
did you have a few days
to just enjoy yourself

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:46.532
when you were there,
or was it right at the tail end?

00:02:46.532 --> 00:02:49.402
-No, I actually
never have had a chance

00:02:49.402 --> 00:02:51.737
to celebrate after the Olympics.

00:02:51.737 --> 00:02:53.872
It's usually straight back
into competition.

00:02:53.872 --> 00:02:58.845
And I think when I won, I think
I had one glass of champagne,

00:02:58.845 --> 00:03:00.480
and then I had a race
the next day.

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:02.781
So there is literally
no celebration

00:03:02.781 --> 00:03:04.617
until the end of the season,
which was March,

00:03:04.617 --> 00:03:07.487
and that's definitely
anticlimactic.

00:03:07.487 --> 00:03:10.089
But I -- you know,
I'll definitely be celebrating

00:03:10.089 --> 00:03:12.491
for the athletes
this time around.

00:03:12.491 --> 00:03:13.892
-All right.
I can't imagine --

00:03:13.892 --> 00:03:15.895
I don't think it's easy
to go on a book tour,

00:03:15.895 --> 00:03:17.297
but I do want
to stress to you --

00:03:17.297 --> 00:03:18.931
and I've met a lot of authors --

00:03:18.931 --> 00:03:21.167
you can drink all the way
through your book tour.

00:03:21.167 --> 00:03:22.902
Please don't wait
till the very end

00:03:22.902 --> 00:03:24.437
and just have
one glass of champagne.

00:03:24.437 --> 00:03:25.672
-Okay, okay.
Great advice, Seth.

00:03:25.672 --> 00:03:26.872
I'll take your word for it.

00:03:26.872 --> 00:03:27.974
I'll definitely try that.

00:03:27.974 --> 00:03:29.909
-A lot of authors
are, like, famous drunks.

00:03:29.909 --> 00:03:32.478
You could even go --
just go on, go for it.

00:03:32.478 --> 00:03:33.746
-Well, I mean, it's just --

00:03:33.746 --> 00:03:35.113
-You've got
a lot of catching up to do.

00:03:35.113 --> 00:03:38.751
-I got après-ski, and I got the,
you know, book tour.

00:03:38.751 --> 00:03:42.387
Is there a term for, like,
drinking on book tours?

00:03:42.387 --> 00:03:43.456
-We're going to come up
with one.

00:03:43.456 --> 00:03:45.591
Yeah, like, après-livre.

00:03:45.591 --> 00:03:48.994
I think that's -- livre,
like the French -- après--

00:03:48.994 --> 00:03:50.396
We'll figure it out.
But I want to ask --

00:03:50.396 --> 00:03:51.564
-That sounds fancy.
That sounds fancy.

00:03:51.564 --> 00:03:52.765
-You -- [ Laughs ]

00:03:52.765 --> 00:03:55.234
Just book beers.
How about book beers?

00:03:55.234 --> 00:03:56.836
-[ Laughs ] Sounds great.

00:03:56.836 --> 00:03:58.871
Let's have some book beers.

00:03:58.871 --> 00:03:59.939
-There you go.

00:03:59.939 --> 00:04:02.107
So you -- you wrote your book,

00:04:02.107 --> 00:04:05.277
and then you actually decided
to go back

00:04:05.277 --> 00:04:07.346
and rework a fair amount of it.

00:04:07.346 --> 00:04:08.948
And I would imagine --

00:04:08.948 --> 00:04:10.249
I'm surprised that doesn't
happen more often.

00:04:10.249 --> 00:04:11.583
You're writing about your life,

00:04:11.583 --> 00:04:13.786
and then when it's finally
on the page and, you know,

00:04:13.786 --> 00:04:15.621
it has a sense
of being the permanent record,

00:04:15.621 --> 00:04:17.323
you must have --

00:04:17.323 --> 00:04:18.524
It's in that moment
that you must think,

00:04:18.524 --> 00:04:19.892
"Wait, this isn't quite right."

00:04:19.892 --> 00:04:22.561
What did you have to go back
and sort of rework

00:04:22.561 --> 00:04:24.597
before it was ready to go?

00:04:24.597 --> 00:04:26.299
-Pretty much everything.

00:04:26.299 --> 00:04:27.734
You know, when I was writing it,

00:04:27.734 --> 00:04:30.603
I was still kind of
closing out my career,

00:04:30.603 --> 00:04:33.272
and then, you know,
right after I retired,

00:04:33.272 --> 00:04:35.308
I was just in
a really weird spot.

00:04:35.308 --> 00:04:38.177
You know, I was definitely
a bit depressed,

00:04:38.177 --> 00:04:41.480
and I, you know, had a bit of
resentment that I had to retire

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:44.450
because I would have loved
to keep going.

00:04:44.450 --> 00:04:48.354
I just physically couldn't do it
anymore with all of my injuries.

00:04:48.354 --> 00:04:51.457
So I just -- The tone
of the book wasn't right,

00:04:51.457 --> 00:04:54.260
and I wanted it to be
really inspirational.

00:04:54.260 --> 00:04:55.561
I wanted it to be, you know,

00:04:55.561 --> 00:04:58.630
motivating to others
who also faced adversity.

00:04:58.630 --> 00:05:00.599
So I literally scrapped
the whole thing

00:05:00.599 --> 00:05:02.034
and started it completely over.

00:05:02.034 --> 00:05:04.603
And now I'm really happy
with the result.

00:05:04.603 --> 00:05:07.206
I think it was
the right decision.

00:05:07.206 --> 00:05:09.508
-I have one last thing
I want to ask you about,

00:05:09.508 --> 00:05:11.677
which is very cool, as well --
not just an author,

00:05:11.677 --> 00:05:15.581
but you also co-directed a film
about one of your heroes,

00:05:15.581 --> 00:05:17.683
Olympic skier Picabo Street.

00:05:17.683 --> 00:05:20.219
How did that come about,
and what was it about her

00:05:20.219 --> 00:05:23.389
that you wanted to --
to make a film?

00:05:23.389 --> 00:05:26.158
-I mean, Picabo has such
an amazing story,

00:05:26.158 --> 00:05:28.094
and I think people know
quite a bit about her

00:05:28.094 --> 00:05:29.595
because she was so successful,

00:05:29.595 --> 00:05:32.898
and that's one of the reasons
why she was my idol growing up.

00:05:32.898 --> 00:05:36.202
But it was such an honor
to be able to direct

00:05:36.202 --> 00:05:38.404
and produce this film
with Frank Marshall.

00:05:38.404 --> 00:05:40.973
I mean, he's such
a legend in film.

00:05:40.973 --> 00:05:44.944
So now everyone will know
all that I know about Picabo.

00:05:44.944 --> 00:05:47.613
And, yeah,
she's just such a legend,

00:05:47.613 --> 00:05:49.715
so it was just truly an honor.

00:05:49.715 --> 00:05:52.518
-Now, I would imagine you --
when you spoke to her

00:05:52.518 --> 00:05:54.286
or when you speak to her,
you always say,

00:05:54.286 --> 00:05:56.889
"You were my hero.
I watched you growing up."

00:05:56.889 --> 00:05:58.858
Are there Olympic athletes
who have started

00:05:58.858 --> 00:06:00.392
saying that to you yet, Lindsey?

00:06:00.392 --> 00:06:02.829
Has that moment come?

00:06:02.829 --> 00:06:05.030
-Yeah, you know, I'm not
that young anymore, Seth,

00:06:05.030 --> 00:06:09.234
so that time has
definitely come, come and gone.

00:06:09.234 --> 00:06:13.639
But I think the person that's
had the most impact on me

00:06:13.639 --> 00:06:14.974
was Sofia Goggia,

00:06:14.974 --> 00:06:18.678
who actually won the last
Olympic downhill in Pyeongchang,

00:06:18.678 --> 00:06:20.880
and I obviously was
in those Olympics with her.

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:23.549
And she's always said
since the beginning

00:06:23.549 --> 00:06:25.417
that I was her idol,
which is a little bit weird

00:06:25.417 --> 00:06:28.020
'cause we're not
that far apart in age,

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:31.056
but it was -- it was
really meaningful to me,

00:06:31.056 --> 00:06:33.392
and she still asked me
for advice.

00:06:33.392 --> 00:06:36.929
And, you know, she's obviously
still at the top of her game.

00:06:36.929 --> 00:06:38.797
She's won every single downhill
this year.

00:06:38.797 --> 00:06:43.001
So to have someone like that,
you know, still having me

00:06:43.001 --> 00:06:47.005
as their idol
is very, very meaningful to me.

00:06:47.005 --> 00:06:48.373
-Well, it's very well deserved.

00:06:48.373 --> 00:06:49.942
Thank you so much
for being here.

00:06:49.942 --> 00:06:52.044
Congratulations on the book
and the film

00:06:52.044 --> 00:06:53.912
and the incredible career.

00:06:53.912 --> 00:06:56.215
I hope next time,
I see you in person.

00:06:56.215 --> 00:06:57.583
-Definitely.
Thanks, Seth.

00:06:57.583 --> 00:06:58.784
Have some book beers for me.

00:06:58.784 --> 00:07:00.553
-[ Laughs ] Will do.

00:07:00.553 --> 00:07:04.023
"Rise: My Story" is on sale now
wherever you buy your books,

00:07:04.023 --> 00:07:05.991
but please support local
and independent bookstores.

00:07:05.991 --> 00:07:07.527
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

